#: processes/document-ingestion.bpmn:81
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "ONE OF TWO ENTRY POINTS, and this said \"the only\" one until 2026-09-20. A drop during an editing session triggers ingestion in the background; the contributor does not wait for it. THE OTHER is materialization: an asset listed in a remote graph that this instance, or one of its dependencies, declares in `remoteGraphs`. Owner, 2026-09-20: \"things can enter library through uploads/ -> library/ document ingestion or through retrieval / materialization of asset known through listed external KG in one of the dependent harnesses.\" That path runs materialize-remote.bpmn FIRST -- purpose, then the five gates -- and re-enters this process at \"Extract structure\" with a file that already carries its provenance and its fixity. It does NOT pass through uploads/, and that is deliberate: uploads/ is a queue for things somebody dropped, and a materialized asset was never dropped. Recording it as an upload would lose the one fact that distinguishes the two, which is that somebody decided it could be held. RENAMED 2026-09-30, and the id is deliberately unchanged. This event was named \"A file lands in uploads/\" — an ARRIVAL, which is why the owner could say the chain's first link was missing: the process began after the file was already in the queue, so nothing in it modelled, governed or documented how the file got there. Task_Place is that step, and an event named for the arrival could not sit in front of it without contradicting it. The id stays `StartEvent_Dropped` because it is referenced from `skills/folio-core/content-acquisition.md` and from five translation catalogues, and an id is an identifier rather than a description."
msgstr ""

#: processes/document-ingestion.bpmn:214
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Owner, 2026-09-24: \"Make as QA sidecar as part of general doc ingestion to slowly drain.\" Nothing is written to ENQUEUE a block: the queue is derived (every prose block in every declared library, minus those whose summaries.json record is a current draft or confirmation), so a promoted entry is in it the moment its blocks are. A re-ingested document whose text changed re-enters it on its own, because the record's source_hash no longer matches. What an agent doing ingestion work does here is DRAIN a few: `bun run summaries:next -- --n K` hands it the next K blocks with their text, it writes a short summary of each in its own words, and `bun run summaries:record` writes them into library/<bib-slug>/summaries.json as drafts naming the agent and its model. The block itself stays verbatim and `ingested`. ADVISORY, never a gate: this step does not hold up Task_Citeable, and check:l1-complete reports the backlog (`block-summaries`) without failing on it. Confirming or rejecting a draft is a person's act, in `bun run narratives`."
msgstr ""

